--TEST--
Core\Random::float's half-open [0, 1) is asserted at both ends — 4000 draws fill all ten tenths of the interval, none falls below 0.0, and none reaches 1.0 — spec § 11, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R5

--FILE--
<?nvs
// `float()` takes no argument, so the *whole* of its contract is the interval
// it draws from — and `[0, 1)` is two claims, neither of which one draw can
// show: nothing below `0.0`, and `1.0` itself never produced. The sibling
// sweep (`random-every-draw-is-swept-for-its-invariants`) counts membership in
// the interval and that both halves are reached; membership alone is held by a
// generator drawing from any sub-interval, so what is counted here is *reach*
// — the two ends named together, which is what makes this a bound and not a
// range check.
int $drawn = 0;
int $inside = 0;
int $filled = 0;
float $lowest = 1.0;
float $highest = 0.0;

// Ten tenths, 400 draws each. A tenth left empty is a `0.9 ** 400` event —
// under 1 in 10^18 — so "every tenth was reached" is an assertion about the
// generator and not a hope about this run. The end tenths are the two that
// matter: `[0.0, 0.1)` is only reachable if the interval is closed at the
// bottom, and `[0.9, 1.0)` only if it runs all the way up to the value it
// must never answer.
for (int $k = 0; $k < 10; $k = $k + 1) {
    float $lo = $k * 0.1;
    float $hi = $lo + 0.1;
    int $hits = 0;
    for (int $i = 0; $i < 400; $i = $i + 1) {
        var $f = Core\Random::float();
        $drawn = $drawn + 1;
        $inside = $inside + ((($f >= 0.0) && ($f < 1.0)) ? 1 : 0);
        $lowest = ($f < $lowest) ? $f : $lowest;
        $highest = ($f > $highest) ? $f : $highest;
        $hits = $hits + ((($f >= $lo) && ($f < $hi)) ? 1 : 0);
    }
    $filled = $filled + (($hits > 0) ? 1 : 0);
}
echo "tenths ", $filled, "\n";
echo "inside ", $inside, " of ", $drawn, "\n";

// The two ends, said one at a time. A drawn value cannot be echoed, so each
// end is rendered as the predicate that names it: the floor is *inclusive* and
// so `>= 0.0` is the comparison it admits, while the ceiling is *exclusive*
// and `< 1.0` is the one it admits — a member that clamped to `[0, 1]`
// instead holds every count above and fails this line alone, once in 2^53
// draws. That last part is why the near-miss counts sit beside them: the
// extremes are approached to within a twentieth from both sides, so an
// implementation buying "never 1.0" by narrowing the interval is caught here
// rather than passing on the strict half.
echo ($lowest >= 0.0) ? "floor held" : "below zero", "\n";
echo ($highest < 1.0) ? "ceiling held" : "reached one", "\n";
echo ($lowest < 0.05) ? "low reached" : "low missed", "\n";
echo ($highest >= 0.95) ? "high reached" : "high missed", "\n";

// Why the exclusive end is the half worth spending a case on: the half-open
// interval is what makes "scale it into my own range" total. `$f * 6.0` is a
// die roll's worth of buckets exactly while `1.0` is unreachable; a single
// `1.0` would land a seventh bucket on a six-sided die.
echo (Core\Random::float() * 6.0 < 6.0) ? "scales" : "overflows", "\n";

--EXPECT--
tenths 10
inside 4000 of 4000
floor held
ceiling held
low reached
high reached
scales
